    [['a1-%d' % i, 'a2-%d' % i] for i in range(n)],\n        [['b1-%d' % i, 'b2-%d' % i] for i in range(n)],\n    ]\n    y = pd.concat([pd.DataFrame(a) for a in x], keys=['X', 'Y']).set_index(1, append=True)\n    print(y)\n\nfunc(5) # no problem\nfunc(50) # bug!\n```\n\nWhen DataFrames are short (in this case, 10 rows), there is no problem.\nHowever, when DataFrames is long (in this case, 100 rows) and omitted middle-part on printing, some columns of index is not correct. \n\n```\n             0\n    1         \nX 0 a2-0  a1-0\n  1 a2-1  a1-1\n  2 a2-2  a1-2\n  3 a2-3  a1-3\n  4 a2-4  a1-4\nY 0 b2-0  b1-0\n  1 b2-1  b1-1\n  2 b2-2  b1-2\n  3 b2-3  b1-3\n  4 b2-4  b1-4\n                   0\n        1           \nX a2-0  a2-0    a1-0\n  a2-1  a2-1    a1-1\n  a2-2  a2-2    a1-2\n  a2-3  a2-3    a1-3\n  a2-4  a2-4    a1-4\n  a2-5  a2-5    a1-5\n  a2-6  a2-6    a1-6\n  a2-7  a2-7    a1-7\n  a2-8  a2-8    a1-8\n  a2-9  a2-9    a1-9\n...              ...\nY b2-40 b2-40  b1-40\n  b2-41 b2-41  b1-41\n  b2-42 b2-42  b1-42\n  b2-43 b2-43  b1-43\n  b2-44 b2-44  b1-44\n  b2-45 b2-45  b1-45\n  b2-46 b2-46  b1-46\n  b2-47 b2-47  b1-47\n  b2-48 b2-48  b1-48\n  b2-49 b2-49  b1-49\n\n[100 rows x 1 columns]\n```\n\nresult of pd.show_versions() is as follows.\n\n```\nINSTALLED VERSIONS\n------------------\ncommit: None\npython: 3.5.1.final.0\npython-bits: 64\nOS: Linux\nOS-release: 2.6.32-279.22.1.el6.x86_64\nmachine: x86_64\nprocessor: x86_64\nbyteorder: little\nLC_ALL: None\nLANG: en_US.UTF-8\n\npandas: 0.17.1\nnose: 1.3.7\npip: 7.1.2\nsetuptools: 18.5\nCython: 0.23.4\nnumpy: 1.10.1\nscipy: 0.16.0\nstatsmodels: 0.6.1\nIPython: 4.0.1\nsphinx: 1.3.1\npatsy: 0.4.0\ndateutil: 2.4.2\npytz: 2015.7\nblosc: None\nbottleneck: 1.0.0\ntables: 3.2.2\nnumexpr: 2.4.4\nmatplotlib: 1.5.0\nopenpyxl: 2.2.6\nxlrd: 0.9.4\nxlwt: 1.0.0\nxlsxwriter: 0.7.7\nlxml: 3.4.4\nbs4: 4.4.1\nhtml5lib: None\nhttplib2: None\napiclient: None\nsqlalchemy: 1.0.9\npymysql: None\npsycopg2: None\nJinja2: None\n```\n","close